Hand derivation (ISA 0.9, independent qjumpr address and pc widths):
The containing instance has rows 0=qjumpr a, 1=qjumpr b, 2=lift.retval x;
HALT=3, so the minimal shared pc/successor width is P=2. Declared address
widths A=3 and A=4 are both legal because 2^A>3. The r1 calls bind
CopyWidth=min(A,P)=2, F=4, First=0, End=3. They compare all A address bits,
then XOR the low two bits into succ; an invalid address still selects HALT
and effective cause 4. Thus addresses 4 and 8 cannot alias row 0 silently.

Input (a,b,x)=(1,2,1) visits rows 0,1,2 and returns payload 1/code 0 in
three steps. Address a=2 reaches the return directly. Address a=0 legally
loops at row 0 and exhausts a finite step budget. An address a>=3 faults
on the first row; when a=1, b>=3 faults on the second row. The interface
inputs are preserved; target/fault delivery is XOR, and inverse traversal
restores every private bit. The source-constructor regression enumerates
all 8*16*2=256 input tuples at B=3 with nonzero initial target/fault values.

The original catalog's single-P qjumpr signature cannot bind both declared
address widths; explicit original-hash compatibility is tested separately
on a uniformly two-bit source. This witness's stored root interface hash
is 36b366835ee485cc34ce608de846f422da6fb69c0e1482ddfbea9ca691c2d863.
